  // Player1 is the player with the fewest matches
  const Player1 = sortedData[0];

  // Select a random player from the remaining players
  const remainingPlayers = sortedData.slice(1);
  const Player2 = remainingPlayers[Math.floor(Math.random() * remainingPlayers.length)];

  // Now you can use Player1 and Player2
  // Rest of your function...
}
```

This code works as follows:

- It sorts the `data` array by the `Matches` property in ascending order. This puts the player with the fewest matches at the beginning of the array.
- It assigns the first player in the sorted array (i.e., the player with the fewest matches) to `Player1`.
- It creates a new array `remainingPlayers` that contains all players except `Player1`.
- It selects a random player from `remainingPlayers` and assigns it to `Player2`.

Note that this code assumes that there are at least two players in the `data` array. If there's only one player, `Player2` will be undefined. You might want to add error checking code to handle this case.

## Computing the N-th Fibonacci Number Mod P

The Fibonacci sequence is a series of numbers in which each number is the sum of the two preceding ones, usually starting with 0 and 1. However, for large values of `n`, direct computation can be inefficient due to the repetitive nature of the calculations. 

Here's a JavaScript function using the matrix exponentiation method to efficiently calculate the `n`-th Fibonacci number modulo `p`.

### Code

```javascript
/**
 * Compute the n-th Fibonacci number modulo p.
 * @param {number} n - The index of the Fibonacci number to compute.
 * @param {number} p - The modulo to apply to the result.
 * @returns {number} The n-th Fibonacci number modulo p.
 */
function fibonacciModP(n, p) {
    // Base cases
    if (n <= 1) return n;

    // Create the base matrix [[1, 1], [1, 0]]
    let baseMatrix = [[1, 1], [1, 0]];

    // Compute the (n-1)-th power of the base matrix modulo p
    let resultMatrix = matrixPower(baseMatrix, n - 1, p);

    // The result is the top-left element of the resulting matrix
    return resultMatrix[0][0];
}

/**
 * Compute the power of a matrix modulo p.
 * @param {number[][]} matrix - The matrix to exponentiate.
 * @param {number} power - The exponent.
 * @param {number} p - The modulo to apply to the result.
 * @returns {number[][]} The matrix raised to the given power modulo p.
 */
function matrixPower(matrix, power, p) {
    // Base case: power of 1
    if (power === 1) return matrix;

    // If the power is even, compute the power of the matrix squared
    if (power % 2 === 0) {
        let squaredMatrix = matrixMultiply(matrix, matrix, p);
        return matrixPower(squaredMatrix, power / 2, p);
    }

    // If the power is odd, compute the power of the matrix squared times the base matrix
    let squaredMatrix = matrixMultiply(matrix, matrix, p);
    return matrixMultiply(matrix, matrixPower(squaredMatrix, (power - 1) / 2, p), p);
}

/**
 * Multiply two matrices modulo p.
 * @param {number[][]} a - The first matrix.
 * @param {number[][]} b - The second matrix.
 * @param {number} p - The modulo to apply to the result.
 * @returns {number[][]} The product of the two matrices modulo p.
 */
function matrixMultiply(a, b, p) {
    let result = [[0, 0], [0, 0]];
    for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        for (let j = 0; j < 2; j++) {
            for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {
                result[i][j] += a[i][k] * b[k][j];
                result[i][j] %= p;
            }
        }
    }
    return result;
}
```

### Example Usage

```javascript
console.log(fibonacciModP(10, 100)); // Output: 55
```

This function works by using the property of Fibonacci numbers that each number is the sum of the two preceding ones. By representing this relationship as a matrix, we can efficiently compute the `n`-th Fibonacci number by exponentiating the matrix. The result is then taken modulo `p` to ensure it stays within the desired range.

