**Extracting Text from a Document using JavaScript**
=====================================================

Below is an example of a JavaScript function that extracts the text from a document. This function uses the `DOMParser` API to parse the HTML of the document and then extracts the text content.

```javascript
/**
 * Extracts the text from a document.
 * 
 * @param {string} html - The HTML of the document.
 * @returns {string} The text content of the document.
 */
function extractTextFromDocument(html) {
    // Create a DOMParser instance
    const parser = new DOMParser();

    // Parse the HTML
    const doc = parser.parseFromString(html, 'text/html');

    // Remove all script and style elements
    const scriptElements = doc.querySelectorAll('script, style');
    scriptElements.forEach((element) => {
        element.remove();
    });

    // Extract the text content
    const text = doc.body.textContent;

    // Return the text content
    return text.trim();
}

// Example usage:
const html = `
    <html>
        <body>
            <h1>Example Document</h1>
            <p>This is an example document.</p>
            <script>alert('This script will be removed');</script>
        </body>
    </html>
`;

const extractedText = extractTextFromDocument(html);
console.log(extractedText);
// Output:
// Example Document
// This is an example document.
```

**Alternative Method using Regular Expressions**
----------------------------------------------

Alternatively, you can use regular expressions to extract the text from a document. This approach is simpler but may not be as accurate as the previous method.

```javascript
/**
 * Extracts the text from a document using regular expressions.
 * 
 * @param {string} html - The HTML of the document.
 * @returns {string} The text content of the document.
 */
function extractTextFromDocumentRegex(html) {
    // Remove all script and style tags
    const cleanedHtml = html.replace(/<script.*?>.*?<\/script>|<style.*?>.*?<\/style>/g, '');

    // Extract the text content
    const text = cleanedHtml.replace(/<.*?>/g, '');

    // Return the text content
    return text.trim();
}

// Example usage:
const html = `
    <html>
        <body>
            <h1>Example Document</h1>
            <p>This is an example document.</p>
            <script>alert('This script will be removed');</script>
        </body>
    </html>
`;

const extractedText = extractTextFromDocumentRegex(html);
console.log(extractedText);
// Output:
// Example Document
// This is an example document.
```

Note that this alternative method may not work correctly if the HTML contains complex nesting or malformed tags. The first method using `DOMParser` is generally more reliable.

Here's a simple Java function that uses Java Streams to check if a list has not more than one entry. If the list has more than one entry, it fires an exception. If the list has exactly one entry, it returns the result. If the list has no entry, it returns null.

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class Main {

    /**
     * Returns the single element of the list, or null if the list is empty.
     * Throws an exception if the list has more than one element.
     *
     * @param list the list to check
     * @param <T> the type of elements in the list
     * @return the single element of the list, or null if the list is empty
     */
    public static <T> T getSingleElement(List<T> list) {
        return list.stream()
                .findFirst()
                .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalArgumentException("List has more than one element"));
    }

    /**
     * Alternative implementation using findFirst() and findAny() methods
     */
    public static <T> T getSingleElementAlternative(List<T> list) {
        return list.stream()
                .findFirst()
                .filter(element -> list.stream().count() == 1)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalArgumentException("List has more than one element"));
    }

    /**
     * Yet another implementation using findAny() and count() methods
     */
    public static <T> T getSingleElementYetAnother(List<T> list) {
        if (list.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }
        if (list.stream().count() > 1) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("List has more than one element");
        }
        return list.stream().findAny().orElse(null);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> list1 = List.of("A");
        System.out.println(getSingleElement(list1)); // prints: A

        List<String> list2 = List.of();
        System.out.println(getSingleElement(list2)); // prints: null

        List<String> list3 = List.of("A", "B");
        try {
            System.out.println(getSingleElement(list3));
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage()); // prints: List has more than one element
        }
    }
}
```

However, I would like to propose a more idiomatic and explicit solution, one that doesn't rely on the "side-effect" of the stream's laziness to handle the "more than one element" case:

```java
public static <T> T getSingleElement(List<T> list) {
    if (list.isEmpty()) {
        return null;
    }
    if (list.size() > 1) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("List has more than one element");
    }
    return list.get(0);
}
```

This solution clearly conveys the intent of the function and handles the different cases in a straightforward manner, avoiding the potential confusion of using streams for this specific task.

Here's a basic implementation of the Kalman filter in GNU Octave. This code assumes that you have a linear system with a state transition model and a measurement model.

```octave
function [x_est, P_est] = kalman_filter(x0, P0, A, B, C, Q, R, u, z)
  % Initialize the state estimate and covariance
  x_est = zeros(size(x0, 1), size(z, 2));
  P_est = zeros(size(P0, 1), size(P0, 2), size(z, 2));

  % Set the initial state estimate and covariance
  x_est(:, 1) = x0;
  P_est(:, :, 1) = P0;

  % Loop through each time step
  for i = 2:size(z, 2)
    % Predict the state and covariance
    x_pred = A * x_est(:, i-1) + B * u(:, i-1);
    P_pred = A * P_est(:, :, i-1) * A' + Q;

    % Compute the innovation
    innov = z(:, i) - C * x_pred;

    % Compute the innovation covariance
    S = C * P_pred * C' + R;

    % Compute the Kalman gain
    K = P_pred * C' / S;

    % Update the state estimate and covariance
    x_est(:, i) = x_pred + K * innov;
    P_est(:, :, i) = (eye(size(P_pred)) - K * C) * P_pred;
  end
end
```

Here's an example of how to use this function:

```octave
% Define the system parameters
A = [1 1; 0 1];  % State transition matrix
B = [0; 1];      % Control input matrix
C = [1 0];       % Measurement matrix
Q = [0.01 0; 0 0.01];  % Process noise covariance
R = 0.1;         % Measurement noise covariance

% Define the initial state and covariance
x0 = [0; 0];
P0 = [1 0; 0 1];

% Define the control inputs
u = [1; 2; 3; 4; 5];

% Define the measurements
z = [1; 2; 3; 4; 5] + randn(1, size(u, 2));

% Run the Kalman filter
[x_est, P_est] = kalman_filter(x0, P0, A, B, C, Q, R, u, z');

% Plot the results
figure;
plot(x_est(1, :));
xlabel('Time Step');
ylabel('State Estimate');
title('State Estimate vs Time');

figure;
plot(z);
xlabel('Time Step');
ylabel('Measurement');
title('Measurement vs Time');
```

This code defines a simple linear system with a state transition matrix `A`, a control input matrix `B`, a measurement matrix `C`, a process noise covariance `Q`, and a measurement noise covariance `R`. It then defines the initial state and covariance, and generates some noisy measurements. The Kalman filter is run on these measurements, and the resulting state estimates are plotted against the measurements.

Note that this is a very basic implementation of the Kalman filter, and may not work for all systems. You may need to modify the code to suit your specific needs.

