PHP 8.2 supports the use of coroutines through the Fiber API. To manage the maximum number of running coroutines, you can use a concept called a "semaphore" or a "mutex" (short for "mutual exclusion"). 

Here's a simple example of how you can use a semaphore to limit the number of running coroutines:

```php
class CoroutineManager {
    private int $maxCoroutines;
    private int $runningCoroutines = 0;
    private array $queue = [];

    public function __construct(int $maxCoroutines) {
        $this->maxCoroutines = $maxCoroutines;
    }

    public function run(\Fiber $fiber): void {
        if ($this->runningCoroutines >= $this->maxCoroutines) {
            $this->queue[] = $fiber;
            Fiber::suspend();
        }

        $this->runningCoroutines++;
        Fiber::resume($fiber);
    }

    public function finish(): void {
        $this->runningCoroutines--;
        if (!empty($this->queue)) {
            $fiber = array_shift($this->queue);
            Fiber::resume($fiber);
        }
    }
}

function task(int $id): void {
    echo "Task $id started\n";
    Fiber::suspend();
    echo "Task $id finished\n";
}

$manager = new CoroutineManager(5);
$fibers = [];

for ($i = 0; $i < 10; $i++) {
    $fibers[] = Fiber::create(function (int $id) use ($manager): void {
        $manager->run(Fiber::getCurrent());
        task($id);
        $manager->finish();
    }, $i);
}

foreach ($fibers as $fiber) {
    Fiber::resume($fiber);
}
```

In this example, the `CoroutineManager` class manages the number of running coroutines by maintaining a counter of running coroutines and a queue of waiting coroutines. When a coroutine is finished, it notifies the manager, which then resumes the next waiting coroutine.

However, please note that this is a very basic example. In real-world applications, you might want to handle errors, implement a timeout mechanism, or use a more advanced data structure like a priority queue. 

Also, note that the PHP Fiber API is still experimental and might not be suitable for production use yet.

To prevent XPath injection, you should use parameterized XPath expressions or escape any user input. Here's an example using JAXP (Java API for XML Processing) and parameterized XPath expressions:

```java
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPath;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathConstants;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathExpressionException;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathFactory;

import org.w3c.dom.Document;
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;

public class XPathExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String user = "username";
        String pass = "password";

        // Use parameterized XPath expression
        String expression = "/users/user[@name=$user and @pass=$pass]";

        // Create an XPath instance
        XPath xPath = XPathFactory.newInstance().newXPath();

        // Create an XPath evaluator
        DocumentBuilderFactory factory = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
        factory.setNamespaceAware(true);
        Document document = factory.newDocumentBuilder().parse("your_xml_file.xml");

        // Create a variable resolver to bind the variables
        VariableResolver resolver = new VariableResolver() {
            @Override
            public Object resolveVariable(QName variableName) {
                if (variableName.getLocalPart().equals("user")) {
                    return user;
                } else if (variableName.getLocalPart().equals("pass")) {
                    return pass;
                } else {
                    return null;
                }
            }
        };

        // Evaluate the XPath expression with the variables
        NodeList nodeList = (NodeList) xPath.evaluate(expression, document, resolver, XPathConstants.NODESET);

        // Process the result
        for (int i = 0; i < nodeList.getLength(); i++) {
            System.out.println(nodeList.item(i).getTextContent());
        }
    }
}

class VariableResolver implements XPathVariableResolver {
    @Override
    public Object resolveVariable(QName variableName) {
        // Implement the logic to resolve the variable
        return null;
    }
}
```

However, JAXP does not directly support parameterized XPath expressions. You can use other libraries like JXPath or Saxon which support parameterized XPath expressions.

Alternatively, you can manually escape any user input:

```java
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPath;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathConstants;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathExpressionException;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathFactory;

import org.w3c.dom.Document;
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;

public class XPathExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String user = "username";
        String pass = "password";

        // Escape any user input
        user = escapeXPath(user);
        pass = escapeXPath(pass);

        // Use the escaped input
        String expression = "/users/user[@name='" + user + "' and @pass='" + pass + "']";

        // Create an XPath instance
        XPath xPath = XPathFactory.newInstance().newXPath();

        // Create an XPath evaluator
        DocumentBuilderFactory factory = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
        factory.setNamespaceAware(true);
        Document document = factory.newDocumentBuilder().parse("your_xml_file.xml");

        // Evaluate the XPath expression
        NodeList nodeList = (NodeList) xPath.evaluate(expression, document, XPathConstants.NODESET);

        // Process the result
        for (int i = 0; i < nodeList.getLength(); i++) {
            System.out.println(nodeList.item(i).getTextContent());
        }
    }

    // Method to escape XPath characters
    public static String escapeXPath(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            return "";
        }
        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
            char c = input.charAt(i);
            switch (c) {
                case '\'':
                    result.append("&apos;");
                    break;
                case '"':
                    result.append("&quot;");
                    break;
                case '\\':
                    result.append("\\\\");
                    break;
                default:
                    result.append(c);
                    break;
            }
        }
        return result.toString();
    }
}
```

This manual escaping approach requires care and can be error-prone. It is recommended to use parameterized XPath expressions whenever possible to prevent XPath injection attacks.

**Strategy Pattern in JavaScript: Banking App System**
===========================================================

The Strategy pattern is a behavioral design pattern that turns a set of behaviors into objects and makes them interchangeable inside original context object. In this example, we'll implement a simple banking app system that uses different payment strategies.

### Payment Strategy Interface

```javascript
// paymentStrategy.js
class PaymentStrategy {
  /**
   * Process payment
   * @param {number} amount
   * @returns {string}
   */
  processPayment(amount) {
    throw new Error('Method must be implemented');
  }
}

module.exports = PaymentStrategy;
```

### Concrete Payment Strategies

```javascript
// creditCardStrategy.js
const PaymentStrategy = require('./paymentStrategy');

class CreditCardStrategy extends PaymentStrategy {
  constructor(cardNumber, cvv, expirationDate) {
    super();
    this.cardNumber = cardNumber;
    this.cvv = cvv;
    this.expirationDate = expirationDate;
  }

  processPayment(amount) {
    return `Payment of $${amount} processed using credit card ${this.cardNumber}`;
  }
}

module.exports = CreditCardStrategy;
```

```javascript
// paypalStrategy.js
const PaymentStrategy = require('./paymentStrategy');

class PayPalStrategy extends PaymentStrategy {
  constructor(email) {
    super();
    this.email = email;
  }

  processPayment(amount) {
    return `Payment of $${amount} processed using PayPal account ${this.email}`;
  }
}

module.exports = PayPalStrategy;
```

```javascript
// bankTransferStrategy.js
const PaymentStrategy = require('./paymentStrategy');

class BankTransferStrategy extends PaymentStrategy {
  constructor(accountNumber, routingNumber) {
    super();
    this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
    this.routingNumber = routingNumber;
  }

  processPayment(amount) {
    return `Payment of $${amount} processed using bank transfer to account ${this.accountNumber}`;
  }
}

module.exports = BankTransferStrategy;
```

### Context (Banking App System)

```javascript
// bankingApp.js
const PaymentStrategy = require('./paymentStrategy');
const CreditCardStrategy = require('./creditCardStrategy');
const PayPalStrategy = require('./paypalStrategy');
const BankTransferStrategy = require('./bankTransferStrategy');

class BankingApp {
  constructor(paymentStrategy) {
    this.paymentStrategy = paymentStrategy;
  }

  setPaymentStrategy(paymentStrategy) {
    this.paymentStrategy = paymentStrategy;
  }

  processPayment(amount) {
    return this.paymentStrategy.processPayment(amount);
  }
}

module.exports = BankingApp;
```

### Example Usage

```javascript
// index.js
const BankingApp = require('./bankingApp');
const CreditCardStrategy = require('./creditCardStrategy');
const PayPalStrategy = require('./paypalStrategy');
const BankTransferStrategy = require('./bankTransferStrategy');

// Create a banking app with a credit card payment strategy
const bankingApp = new BankingApp(new CreditCardStrategy('1234-5678-9012-3456', '123', '12/2025'));
console.log(bankingApp.processPayment(100)); // Payment of $100 processed using credit card 1234-5678-9012-3456

// Switch to PayPal payment strategy
bankingApp.setPaymentStrategy(new PayPalStrategy('user@example.com'));
console.log(bankingApp.processPayment(50)); // Payment of $50 processed using PayPal account user@example.com

// Switch to bank transfer payment strategy
bankingApp.setPaymentStrategy(new BankTransferStrategy('1234567890', '123456789'));
console.log(bankingApp.processPayment(200)); // Payment of $200 processed using bank transfer to account 1234567890
```

In this example, the `BankingApp` class uses a payment strategy to process payments. The payment strategy is interchangeable, allowing the banking app to switch between different payment methods (credit card, PayPal, bank transfer) without modifying its internal logic.

Here's a draft program that should help you complete the pre-lab assignment. This program uses the `addsubroutine` to add the last two values pushed onto the stack and then uses this subroutine to add all the elements in the `myArray`.

```arm
.data
    .align 4
    myArray: .space 600
    sumOfArray: .word 0

.text
    .global main
    main:
        // Generate myArray using the memorygenerate macro
        memorygenerate myArray, 150

        // Initialize the stack pointer
        mov sp, #0
        add sp, sp, #0x1000 // Assuming a stack size of 0x1000

        // Initialize r0 to the base address of myArray
        ldr r0, =myArray
        mov r1, #0 // Initialize sum to 0

        // Push all elements of myArray onto the stack
        mov r2, #150
        pushLoop:
            ldr r3, [r0], #4
            push {r3}
            subs r2, #1
            bne pushLoop

        // Call the addsubroutine to add all elements on the stack
        mov r0, #149 // Initialize counter to 149 (since we're adding 2 values at a time)
        addSubroutineCallLoop:
            bl addsubroutine
            subs r0, #1
            bne addSubroutineCallLoop

        // Pop the final sum from the stack
        pop {r1} // This will pop the final sum into r1

        // Store the sum in sumOfArray
        ldr r0, =sumOfArray
        str r1, [r0]

        // End of program
        bx lr

    // Subroutine to add the last two values on the stack
    addsubroutine:
        // Save the current state of the registers
        push {r0, r1, r2, r3, lr}

        // Get the last two values from the stack
        pop {r0} // Get the last value from the stack
        pop {r1} // Get the second last value from the stack

        // Add the two values
        add r2, r0, r1

        // Push the result back onto the stack (replacing the second last value)
        push {r2}

        // Restore the current state of the registers
        pop {r0, r1, r2, r3, lr}

        // Return from the subroutine
        bx lr
```

This program first generates the `myArray` using the `memorygenerate` macro. Then it pushes all elements of `myArray` onto the stack. After that, it calls the `addsubroutine` to add all elements on the stack. The final sum is then popped from the stack and stored in `sumOfArray`.

Note that the `addsubroutine` uses a simple approach to add the last two values on the stack. It pops the last two values, adds them, and then pushes the result back onto the stack (replacing the second last value). This approach is repeated until all elements on the stack have been added.

Also note that this program assumes a stack size of 0x1000. You may need to adjust this value depending on your specific requirements.

Here's a basic example of a PERN (PostgreSQL, Express, React, Node.js) stack to-do list app.

**Database (PostgreSQL) Schema**

First, create a new PostgreSQL database and add the following schema:

```sql
CREATE TABLE todos (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    title VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    description TEXT,
    completed BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,
    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);
```

**Backend (Node.js, Express) Code**

Create a new Node.js project and install the required packages:

```bash
npm init
npm install express pg dotenv
```

Create a `.env` file to store your database credentials:

```bash
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_USER=your_username
DB_PASSWORD=your_password
DB_NAME=your_database
```

Create a `database.js` file to connect to your database:

```javascript
// database.js
require('dotenv').config();

const { Pool } = require('pg');

const pool = new Pool({
    user: process.env.DB_USER,
    host: process.env.DB_HOST,
    database: process.env.DB_NAME,
    password: process.env.DB_PASSWORD,
});

module.exports = pool;
```

Create a `server.js` file to handle API requests:

```javascript
// server.js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const pool = require('./database');
const cors = require('cors');

app.use(cors());
app.use(express.json());

// Create a new to-do
app.post('/api/todos', async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const { title, description } = req.body;
        const newTodo = await pool.query(
            'INSERT INTO todos (title, description) VALUES ($1, $2) RETURNING *',
            [title, description]
        );
        res.json(newTodo.rows[0]);
    } catch (err) {
        console.error(err.message);
        res.status(500).json({ message: 'Failed to create to-do' });
    }
});

// Get all to-dos
app.get('/api/todos', async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const allTodos = await pool.query('SELECT * FROM todos');
        res.json(allTodos.rows);
    } catch (err) {
        console.error(err.message);
        res.status(500).json({ message: 'Failed to get to-dos' });
    }
});

// Update a to-do
app.put('/api/todos/:id', async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const id = req.params.id;
        const { title, description, completed } = req.body;
        const updatedTodo = await pool.query(
            'UPDATE todos SET title = $1, description = $2, completed = $3 WHERE id = $4 RETURNING *',
            [title, description, completed, id]
        );
        res.json(updatedTodo.rows[0]);
    } catch (err) {
        console.error(err.message);
        res.status(500).json({ message: 'Failed to update to-do' });
    }
});

// Delete a to-do
app.delete('/api/todos/:id', async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const id = req.params.id;
        await pool.query('DELETE FROM todos WHERE id = $1', [id]);
        res.json({ message: 'To-do deleted successfully' });
    } catch (err) {
        console.error(err.message);
        res.status(500).json({ message: 'Failed to delete to-do' });
    }
});

const port = 5000;
app.listen(port, () => {
    console.log(`Server started on port ${port}`);
});
```

