**iMessage Extension: Two Button Keyboard View**

To create an iMessage extension that displays two buttons in the keyboard view, follow these steps:

### Step 1: Create a new iMessage Extension project in Xcode

* Open Xcode and go to `File` > `New` > `Project...`
* Select `iMessage Extension` under the `Starter Kits` section
* Click `Next`
* Choose `App Clip` or `App` as the type of extension
* Click `Next`
* Enter the project name, team, and organization identifier
* Click `Next`
* Choose the project location and click `Create`

### Step 2: Design the keyboard view

* Open the `MainInterface.storyboard` file
* Drag a `UIViewController` onto the canvas
* Set the `UIViewController` as the initial view controller
* Drag two `UIButton` instances onto the view controller
* Set the button titles to "Button 1" and "Button 2"
* Set the button constraints to center them horizontally and vertically

### Step 3: Implement the keyboard view controller

* Open the `MessagesViewController.swift` file
* Import the `Messages` framework
* Create a new class that conforms to `MSMessagesAppViewController`
* Override the `viewDidLoad()` method to configure the keyboard view

```swift
import UIKit
import Messages

class MessagesViewController: MSMessagesAppViewController {

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()

        // Create a new instance of the keyboard view controller
        let keyboardViewController = KeyboardViewController()

        // Present the keyboard view controller
        presentViewController(keyboardViewController, animated: false, completion: nil)
    }
}

class KeyboardViewController: UIViewController {

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()

        // Create a new instance of the keyboard view
        let keyboardView = UIView(frame: self.view.bounds)
        keyboardView.backgroundColor = .white

        // Add the keyboard view to the view controller
        self.view.addSubview(keyboardView)

        // Create two buttons
        let button1 = UIButton(type: .system)
        button1.setTitle("Button 1", for: .normal)
        button1.addTarget(self, action: #selector(button1Tapped), for: .touchUpInside)

        let button2 = UIButton(type: .system)
        button2.setTitle("Button 2", for: .normal)
        button2.addTarget(self, action: #selector(button2Tapped), for: .touchUpInside)

        // Add the buttons to the keyboard view
        keyboardView.addSubview(button1)
        keyboardView.addSubview(button2)

        // Set the button constraints
        button1.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false
        button2.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false

        NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
            button1.centerXAnchor.constraint(equalTo: keyboardView.centerXAnchor),
            button1.centerYAnchor.constraint(equalTo: keyboardView.centerYAnchor),
            button2.centerXAnchor.constraint(equalTo: keyboardView.centerXAnchor),
            button2.centerYAnchor.constraint(equalTo: keyboardView.centerYAnchor, constant: 50)
        ])
    }

    @objc func button1Tapped() {
        print("Button 1 tapped")
    }

    @objc func button2Tapped() {
        print("Button 2 tapped")
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Configure the Info.plist file

* Open the `Info.plist` file
* Add the `NSExtension` key and set its value to `YES`
* Add the `NSExtensionPointIdentifier` key and set its value to `com.apple.messagesapp`
* Add the `NSExtensionPrincipalClass` key and set its value to `MessagesViewController`

### Step 5: Run the extension

* Build and run the extension on a physical device or simulator
* Open the Messages app and select a conversation
* Tap the "Apps" button to open the app drawer
* Tap the "Your Extension" button to open the keyboard view

The two buttons should now be visible in the keyboard view. Tapping either button will print a message to the console.

To download a webpage directly using the browser console, you can use the following JavaScript code:

```javascript
const url = 'https://example.com'; // replace with the desired URL
const a = document.createElement('a');
a.href = url;
a.download = url.split('/').pop(); // set the filename
document.body.appendChild(a);
a.click();
document.body.removeChild(a);
```

However, this method has some limitations:

* It will not work for all types of files, especially if the browser does not support the file type.
* It will not work if the server does not allow cross-origin requests.
* The filename will be set to the last part of the URL, which might not be what you want.

A more robust way to download a webpage is to use the `fetch` API and the `URL.createObjectURL` method:

```javascript
const url = 'https://example.com'; // replace with the desired URL
fetch(url)
  .then(response => response.blob())
  .then(blob => {
    const filename = url.split('/').pop(); // set the filename
    const link = document.createElement('a');
    link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    link.download = filename;
    document.body.appendChild(link);
    link.click();
    document.body.removeChild(link);
    URL.revokeObjectURL(link.href);
  });
```

This method will work for most types of files and will respect the filename specified in the URL.

Note: These methods will work in most modern browsers, but the behavior may vary depending on the browser and its configuration.

