Below is an example program in Rust that reads URLs from a file, separates YouTube URLs from other URLs, and downloads the YouTube URLs using `yt-dlp`.

**Note:** This program assumes that `yt-dlp` is installed and available in the system's PATH.

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufReader, BufRead};
use std::process::Command;
use url::Url;

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    // Read URLs from a file
    let file = File::open("urls.txt")?;
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);
    let urls: Vec<String> = reader
        .lines()
        .filter_map(|line| line.ok())
        .collect();

    // Separate YouTube URLs from other URLs
    let youtube_urls: Vec<String> = urls
        .into_iter()
        .filter(|url| is_youtube_url(url))
        .collect();
    let other_urls: Vec<String> = urls
        .into_iter()
        .filter(|url| !is_youtube_url(url))
        .collect();

    // Download YouTube URLs using yt-dlp
    download_youtube_urls(&youtube_urls)?;

    // Print other URLs
    println!("Other URLs:");
    for url in other_urls {
        println!("{}", url);
    }

    Ok(())
}

fn is_youtube_url(url: &str) -> bool {
    let parsed_url = Url::parse(url).unwrap();
    parsed_url.host_str() == Some("www.youtube.com") || parsed_url.host_str() == Some("youtube.com")
}

fn download_youtube_urls(urls: &[String]) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    for url in urls {
        println!("Downloading: {}", url);
        let status = Command::new("yt-dlp")
            .arg(url)
            .status()?;
        if !status.success() {
            println!("Failed to download: {}", url);
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

**Usage:**

1. Create a file named `urls.txt` in the same directory as the Rust program.
2. Add URLs to the `urls.txt` file, one per line.
3. Run the Rust program using `cargo run`.
4. The program will download the YouTube URLs using `yt-dlp` and print the other URLs to the console.

**Note:** Make sure to replace `urls.txt` with the actual path to your file if it's different.

